---
import DocsNav from "../components/DocsNav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { repositoryUrl } from "../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Evidence | Loom Infer"
  description="Correctness and performance evidence for permanent Loom Infer providers."
  active="Evidence"
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
    <h1>Each claim has a fixed boundary.</h1>
    <p>Each result fixes the source, contract, hardware, baseline, and method.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="docs-layout">
    <DocsNav active="Evidence" />
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Matched eager performance</h2>
      <p>
        Current records compare attention and fused KV paths with the latest
        stable FlashInfer release, <code>v0.6.16.post1</code>, on one H20.
      </p>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Explicit multi-token append keeps one fused launch.</strong>
        <p>
          Loom accepts 1 through 64 caller-indexed tokens. On the admitted
          six-token shuffled suffix case, its one cuda-oxide kernel records
          5.510 microseconds versus 11.732 microseconds for FlashInfer's
          two-kernel composition, or 2.129x lower latency. Both provider-order
          deltas are below five percent, and all 200 raw samples are retained.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same command captures into one Loom Graph node. Single-replay
          medians are 8.288 microseconds for Loom and 13.728 microseconds for
          FlashInfer's two-node graph, making Loom 1.656x lower-latency.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-flashinfer-v0.6.16.post1-bf16-rope-paged-kv-append-tokens-eager-performance-20260806.json`}>
          Multi-token fused append matched performance result
        </a>
        <br />
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-flashinfer-v0.6.16.post1-bf16-rope-paged-kv-append-tokens-graph-performance-20260806.json`}>
          Multi-token fused append matched Graph result
        </a>
        <br />
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-bf16-rope-paged-kv-append-tokens-correctness-20260806.json`}>
          Multi-token fused append correctness result
        </a>
        <br />
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-bf16-rope-paged-kv-append-tokens-cuda-graph-correctness-20260806.json`}>
          Multi-token fused append Graph correctness result
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Fused RoPE plus paged KV append removes one launch.</strong>
        <p>
          On the admitted batch-4 Q16/K4 D128, page-size-16 case, Loom's one
          cuda-oxide kernel records 3.989 microseconds versus 11.735
          microseconds for FlashInfer's two-kernel composition, or 2.942x
          lower latency under fixed host affinity. Both provider-order deltas
          are below five percent, and all 200 raw samples are retained.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-flashinfer-v0.6.16.post1-bf16-rope-paged-kv-append-eager-performance-20260806.json`}>
          Fused RoPE append matched performance result
        </a>
        <br />
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-bf16-rope-paged-kv-append-correctness-20260806.json`}>
          Fused RoPE append correctness result
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Paged decode has shape-specific winners.</strong>
        <p>
          Eight-warp block-local token parallelism lowers Loom MQA and GQA
          eager latency by 3.78x and 3.32x relative to the direct record. Loom
          is now 4.41x lower-latency for batch-1 MHA and 2.35x lower-latency
          for mixed-length batch-3 MQA than FlashInfer.
        </p>
        <p>
          The batch-4 GQA ranking is excluded because FlashInfer's order delta
          is 60.62%. All 600 current raw samples are retained. CUPTI records
          Loom MQA/GQA kernel medians of 4.864 and 4.928 microseconds.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-flashinfer-v0.6.16.post1-paged-token-parallel-eager-performance-20260806.json`}>
          Paged token-parallel matched performance result
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Parallel merge closes more of the long-context gap.</strong>
        <p>
          Identical BF16 operand bits, preallocated buffers, CUDA events, and
          both provider orders were used. The complete path lowers Loom median
          latency by 5.39x at GQA KV length 127 and 38.19x at KV length 4096
          relative to the recorded direct baseline. FlashInfer remains 1.17x
          and 2.09x lower-latency at those shapes.
        </p>
        <p>
          The record retains 1,400 raw samples. Separate CUPTI activity timing
          records a 3.99x merge-kernel improvement. Hardware counters, CUDA
          Graph, engine, and serving claims remain excluded.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-flashinfer-v0.6.16.post1-parallel-merge-eager-performance-20260805.json`}>
          Parallel-merge matched performance result
        </a>
        <br />
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-bf16-single-decode-parallel-merge-profiling-20260805.json`}>
          Isolated kernel profiling result
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2>Current source projection</h2>
      <p>
        The ragged prefill, paged batch-decode, and parallel-merge records
        independently qualify their declared device paths.
      </p>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>BF16 ragged causal prefill passed its H20 gate.</strong>
        <p>
          Direct, eight/sixteen-warp, and tiled eight-partition NHD D128 MHA,
          MQA, and GQA batches pass the CPU oracle. Separate query/KV indptr
          arrays, explicit tiled workspace, bottom-right causal alignment, and
          metadata guards are covered. Four sanitizer tools report no errors.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-bf16-ragged-prefill-cp-async-correctness-20260806.json`}>
          Ragged prefill correctness result
        </a>
        <p>
          Unrolled 16-byte cp.async K/V staging lowers long-GQA latency to
          48.232 microseconds, 1.148x below the previous tiled path and 7.729x
          below direct. FlashInfer remains 2.206x ahead on stable long GQA.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-flashinfer-v0.6.16.post1-ragged-prefill-cp-async-eager-performance-20260806.json`}>
          Ragged prefill matched performance result
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>BF16 paged batch decode passed its H20 gate.</strong>
        <p>
          NHD D128 page-size-16 MHA, MQA, and GQA batches produce bit-exact
          BF16 output against the CPU oracle. Mixed request lengths, physical
          page reordering and reuse, and an invalid-page guard are covered.
          Four sanitizer tools report no errors.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-bf16-paged-batch-decode-correctness-20260806.json`}>
          Paged batch-decode correctness result
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>BF16 parallel merge passed its H20 gate.</strong>
        <p>
          Tuned MQA and GQA partitions match the CPU reference within the
          declared limits. Four sanitizer tools report no errors. The merge
          uses 4,160 bytes of shared memory and assembles without stack or
          spills.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-bf16-single-decode-parallel-merge-correctness-20260805.json`}>
          Parallel-merge correctness result
        </a>
        <p>
          The earlier direct-decode and shared-command records remain immutable
          historical evidence.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-bf16-single-decode-correctness-20260803.json`}>
          Direct-decode correctness history
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2>Earlier operator records</h2>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Owned bindings and fixed-address Graph replay passed on H20.</strong>
        <p>
          The graph captures BF16 RMSNorm and one fixed BF16 cuBLASLt GEMM.
          It replays twice and produces a bit-exact final output. Compute
          Sanitizer reports no errors or device leaks. Performance is not
          measured.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-owned-bindings-cuda-graph-correctness-20260803.json`}>
          Graph machine-readable result
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>F32 RMSNorm passed its recorded H20 correctness gate.</strong>
        <p>
          Four shapes match the CPU reference, and an invalid buffer is
          rejected. Checked bindings chain two commands on a non-default
          stream with one completion record. The chained maximum absolute
          error is <code>7.152557373e-7</code>. Performance is not measured.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-rms-norm-f32-command-scope-20260802.json`}>
          Machine-readable result
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>FP16 and BF16 RMSNorm passed their recorded H20 correctness gates.</strong>
        <p>
          Scalar and packed paths match the storage-rounded CPU references.
          Three short-buffer positions are rejected. Two launches share one
          completion record without an intermediate host wait. The FP16 chain
          reaches two ULPs, and the BF16 chain reaches one ULP. Performance is
          not measured.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-rms-norm-low-precision-20260802.json`}>
          Low-precision result
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>One fixed BF16 cuBLASLt GEMM plan passed its recorded H20 gate.</strong>
        <p>
          Standalone and transpose-sensitive cases match the CPU reference.
          Short A, W, and D buffers are rejected. BF16 RMSNorm feeds GEMM under
          one completion without an intermediate host wait. The selected H20
          algorithm requires no workspace. Performance is not measured.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-bf16-cublaslt-correctness-20260802.json`}>
          BF16 GEMM result
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2>Evidence levels</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Correctness fixes the contract, oracle, limits, and edge cases.</li>
        <li>Kernel evidence uses matched buffers, streams, and provider order.</li>
        <li>Graph evidence covers capture and replay with fixed plans.</li>
        <li>Engine evidence proves invocation and model output.</li>
        <li>Serving evidence reports latency, throughput, memory, and workload.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Read the
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/README.md`}>result format</a>
        and <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/development/h20-validation.md`}>H20 validation guide</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
